<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pagoda of Fogong Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1056</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sakyamuni Pagoda, Yingxian Wooden Pagoda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pagoda of Fogong Temple, or Sakyamuni Pagoda, at Yingxian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, was built in 1056 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>. About 67 metres high, it is the oldest and tallest fully wooden pagoda surviving in China, built without nails and standing through many earthquakes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pagoda-of-fogong-temple.jpg" alt="Pagoda of Fogong Temple" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charlie fong · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pagodaoffogongtemple2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
